<h2>Can breathing exercises help with stress?</h2>
<p><strong>A gentle breathing exercise can be a simple way to pause and focus.</strong></p>
<p>That's different from promising that cortisol will fall by a certain amount, or within a set number of minutes.</p>

<h2>A simple practice</h2>
<p>The <a href="https://www.nhs.uk/mental-health/self-help/guides-tools-and-activities/breathing-exercises-for-stress/">NHS breathing guide</a> suggests:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Get into a comfortable position with your body supported.</li>
  <li>Breathe gently. Don't force the breath deeper.</li>
  <li>Breathe in through your nose and out through your mouth. Count steadily if it helps.</li>
  <li>Keep going for about five minutes.</li>
</ol>
<p>The goal is to feel comfortable. It's not a race to reach a count, hold your breath longer or force a result.</p>
<p><strong>Stop if you feel unwell.</strong> If you have a condition that affects your breathing, ask your clinician which exercises are right for you.</p>

<ZenTip image="/app-ui/zen_new_breathe.webp">
  Rest one hand on your chest. If your shoulders creep up, <strong>slow down</strong> and breathe a little softer.
</ZenTip>

<h2>Is one named method better than another?</h2>
<p><strong>A method's name is not proof that it works.</strong></p>
<p>Box breathing, longer-exhale breathing and other named methods use different timings. A study of one method or result doesn't show that a different method lowers cortisol.</p>
<p>We have removed our earlier rankings of methods. We also removed promises of a specific drop in heart rate, rise in heart rate variability (HRV) or hormone response.</p>

<h2>What the Cortisol+ exercise includes</h2>
<p>Cortisol+ has an animated timer with:</p>
<ul>
  <li>breathe-in, hold and breathe-out prompts</li>
  <li>a cycle counter</li>
  <li>a button to end early</li>
</ul>
<p>See the <a href="/features/breathing-zen-mode/">guided breathing feature</a> for exactly how the sequence works.</p>
<p>The screen at the end shows that you finished or ended a session. It is not a before-and-after cortisol test.</p>
<p>Before you assume a wearable reading was taken after your practice, check the time it was recorded.</p>

